        "source_occurrence_id": null,
        "source_locator": null,
        "speaker_label": "காண்",
        "speaker_label_origin": "source-explicit",
        "source_delimiter": ":-",
        "page_provenance": [
          {
            "pdf_page": 10,
            "printed_page": 9
          }
        ]
      },
      "translation": {
        "english_text": "Love! The name of the curtain you drew over your lust-mad desire is love! The stench of rot given the name of evening-jasmine fragrance—ah! I was deceived, taking Halahala poison for nectar! I mistook sunset for sunrise and was ruined to the root! If there is such a thing as hell, I would set a snake upon your veins and have it bite you... Even then there would be no settling this deceiver's betrayal of trust. Princess—merciless demon! A Brahma-rakshasi born in a royal line that torments the poor, eats their bones, and polishes its body with them! You bewitched me with your beauty, shattered my young heart with the charm of your words, and left me stranded in the street, destroyer! The fault is not yours! Kings hunger to seize ever-new kingdoms! A king's daughter hungers to seize ever-new husbands!",
        "mode": "prose-faithful",
        "notes": [
          "`ஆலகால விஷம்` is retained as the mythic Halahala poison rather than generalized. The compressed source phrase around `இந்தக் கள்ளியின் நம்பிக்கை நிவர்த்தி` is rendered conservatively as a betrayal-of-trust image rather than silently emended."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
